      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "The purpose of this study was to assess the clinical and angiographic benefits of elective stenting in coronary arteries with a reference diameter of 2.1 to 3.0 mm, as compared with traditional percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty (PTCA)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The problems related to small-vessel stenting might be overcome using modern stents designed for small vessels, combined with effective antiplatelet therapy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "In five centers, 145 patients with stable or unstable angina were randomly assigned to elective stenting treatment with the heparin (Hepamed)-coated beStent or PTCA. Control angiography was performed after six months. The primary end point was the minimal lumen diameter (MLD) at follow-up. Secondary end points were the restenosis rate, event-free survival and angina status."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "At follow-up, there was a trend toward a larger MLD in the stent group (1.69 +/- 0.52 mm vs. 1.57 +/- 0.44 mm, p = 0.096). Event-free survival at follow-up was significantly higher in the stent group: 90.5% vs. 76.1% (p = 0.016). The restenosis rate was low in both groups (9.7% and 18.8% in the stent and PTCA groups, respectively; p = 0.15). Analyzed as treated, both the MLD and restenosis rate were significantly improved in patients who had stents as compared with PTCA."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "In small coronary arteries, both PTCA and elective stenting are associated with good clinical and angiographic outcomes after six months. Compared with PTCA, elective treatment with the heparin-coated beStent improves the clinical outcome; however, there was only a nonsignificant trend toward angiographic improvement."
        }
      ]},
